      "math_academy_id": "q-32511",
      "sequence_position": 17,
      "problem": "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k$ \nand \n$c={e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k.$",
      "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})+{e}^{-2}\\cdot {e}^{2}-{e}^{2}\\cdot (2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & =e-1+1-2e+3 \\\\  & =3-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
